From 81e39e7466baab036f2390181e8b3891d79cdf4a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Marin <andre@alexamarin.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 21:50:39 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] fix(skills): let the roster say *not yet*, so a slow reviewer
 is not read as a dead one
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Step 3's loop had one predicate — is the file there — so a reviewer still
running and a reviewer that came back having written nothing both printed
MISSING. The two need opposite responses: wait, and re-ask. Observed twice; on
the skill's first real run three reports landed across three minutes with the
largest last, and a roster read in that window said two of three.

A second list fixes it. `RETURNED` is what the dispatch told you, not what the
reviewer told you — a reviewer's "I already reported" is the recollection this
step exists to disbelieve, while its run having ended is an observation nobody
has to trust. The roster keeps its literal `for` list on purpose: `for id in
$RETURNED` reads the same and is not, because zsh does not split an unquoted
parameter, and the existing zsh test caught exactly that.

Not a wait or a poll. A fixed sleep before the read makes the race less likely
and no more legible, and `a-rule-is-expressed-as-a-check` rejects a rule whose
violation is invisible in the artifacts — a longer timeout is prose about
timing. Three words the reader can see beats a window they cannot.

`RUNNING` then has to be a failure marker in `_body.py` too, or the word this
change introduces is a hole in a check that already knows MISSING is bad: a
description carrying `RUNNING` is a panel recorded as finished while a reviewer
is still out, which is the 2026-09-06 cost written down and shipped. Each
marker now carries its own remedy, because "ask that reviewer again" sends the
agent after one that is working fine.

The two roster tests merged into one — an absent file and an empty one no
longer mean the same thing, so a fixture covering all three states is the
smaller check, not the larger.

Refs #173

